  based on the fact that there's nothing  perfect in software engineering there's  no perfect line of code there's no  perfect software engineer there's no  perfect application or code base for  those of you who do have experience  diving into existing code bases you know  that there's a lot of dust bunnies and  weird architectural decisions and just  lots of cruft that exists I know that  whenever I start a new job I'm like  your app is great like it's so stable  and it's probably made so well and then  I peek behind the covers and I'm like  what is this miss this makes no sense  and I think you'll just find that at all  the roles nothing is perfect and  software engineering so once you realize  that nothing is perfect then also you  realize that oh it's totally okay to  make mistakes and fail and just learn  from your failures because there's just  so much to learn all the time now it  took a little bit for me to wrap my head  around the fact that I'm always gonna be  failing so I just want to take us back  to like my first job as a software  engineer where I was doing iOS  development now I hadn't really done any  meaningful amount of iOS development  before that like I made an app once with  some people of which I wrote maybe a  couple lines of code so I knew that in  my first role I would need to do a lot  of learning I had no idea how to use  Xcode I barely wrote Objective C and so  that's the thing that I really focused  on in my first couple of years so that's  exactly what I did I told my manager hey  I know nothing please help me learn  something so that I can be a valuable  software engineer and contribute to the  codebase and the team and the  application and I just really wanted to  be exposed to lots of different parts of  iOS development in all the different  areas of software development web  development security gaming development  you can go so so deep into each area and  within each area there's lots of other  little areas there's just the whole  world to learn basically in each thing  and that's exactly what my manager did  he assigned me books to read he told me  some courses to take as well as some  tutorials to do and also I found mentors  within my team who could also guide me  through all of this too and I really  can't emphasize enough how much I didn't  know at the beginning but once I  realized and told myself and told others  and was honest with myself like yo I  don't know stuff that's where the  learning began because I was willing to  accept the fact that you know I'm a  failure right now but I can get myself  out of this by learning new skills and  yes the beginning was very very hard  I remember my very first bug bash which  is worse where we all sit together and  just resolve a bunch of bugs and sitting  there being like I don't I don't know  how to do any of this cuz I don't know  what I'm doing and I can barely read a  gypsy and always being faced with what a  little I knew was tough but those are  the little failure points that I turned  into motivation to learn new skills and  then after a while maybe about six  months to a year I looked back and I was  like you know I actually know some stuff  especially compared to my first week on  the job and I'm really proud of myself  for putting in that work and accepting  the fact that you know I don't know a  whole lot and I need to learn a lot more  and now I do know a lot more and I feel  way more competent as an engineer and I  know a lot more about iOS development I  mean a big part of this is just reaching  out for help and asking people and  telling people like yo help me I don't  know what I'm doing so that was kind of  my experience as an early career  software engineer just absorbing  everything about the technology and now  I'm gonna get to the point where I  mentioned earlier about learning other  skills that are also really important so  after I felt like I had a good grasp of  iOS development maybe that was about  like two years later I realized that  there aren't really other paved paths  past this point it was all just about  figuring out what I wanted to get better  at what I failed at and realizing from  there the skills that I want and need in  order to progress towards a place that I  want to be now this all sounds vague and  ambiguous but really what I'm trying to  say is things like product skills people  skills creativity skills writing skills  these are all important skills to  software engineering that I didn't  realize we're important at the beginning  and actually I would argue that in order  to become a really well-rounded software  engineer it's not just about the  technical skills you need all of the  other skills in order to do a good job  for instance the people skills that I've  worked on have enabled me to talk about  technology to non-technical people and  has really translated to like the  content that I make  on here which I enjoy doing I mean if  we're being really honest if you told me  in my first year working that I would  eventually make a YouTube channel about  software engineering I'd be like no  that's not happening but here we are  because I mean I realized that I just  want to do this and it would be fun so  here I am thanks for subscribing if  you're not subscribed click on the  subscribe button and I realize at this  point when there were all these other  skills that I would need to learn to be  the best person and software engineer I  could be I realized that there's really  a merit to being a lifelong learner as a  software engineer and I think that is  the really important lesson here yes is  the software engineering means you're  always failing but in order to overcome  that you just have to be a lifelong  learner and the best news is anybody can  be a lifelong learner you just have to  have that kind of like I'm okay to fail  I accept that I fail I'm going to figure  out how not to feel the next time and  understand what you need to learn to do  better so